            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, we identified that your house has some air leakage associated with the following locations:



** The outside doors - these doors are not sealing properly (can seal with upgraded weatherstripping and caulking around the perimeter of the interior door trim)

** The electrical outlets &amp; switches in exterior walls of the house. Foam wall plate gaskets can be installed behind the plates..

** Some of the slider windows have leakage along the top of the slider section and where the two frames lock together. These windows are newer and are generally OK, however if you see ice buildup or feel cold drafts, you should check with the manufacturer/supplier regarding updated weather-stripping.

** Around the attic hatch (can seal with removable caulking such as "Draft Attack")



Savings: If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a modest 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss. This is estimated to save $ 183 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 4.8 GJ. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT:



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant between $550 and $1000 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on the level achieved. This is one of the most cost-effective energy-saving measures you can undertake. An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.





</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house (including the 2' shallow space below the main floor on the right side) appear to be 2x6 with R20 batt insulation with 1" (R4) of expanded polystyrene foam board insulation on the outside (behind the newer siding). Most newer houses in Inuvik are being constructed with a minimum R35 exterior walls so (as an FYI) I modeled the energy savings if your house was to have R35 (ie: another R11) on the exterior walls. 



Savings: By increasing your exterior wall insulation up to a minimum R35, you could potentially save $588 in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 15.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program ÂinsulationÂ rebate, a pre-approval is strongly recommended and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval recommendation. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

https://aea.nt.ca/program/energy-efficient-products/

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT:



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of up to $5,000 is available for adding insulation to the exterior wall area of your home. Insulation grants are based on the percentage of wall area to which the insulation has been added and the amount of insulation added. A minimum of 20% of the exterior wall area of your home, excluding foundation walls, must be insulated in order to qualify for a grant.</mainWalls>
                <foundation>

Your crawlspace walls appear to be 2x6 framing with R20 insulation. I have modelled adding 3Â (R15) of rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to the interior of your crawlspace walls.



Savings: By adding 3Â (R15) of rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to the interior of your crawlspace walls, you could potentially save $ 80 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2 GJ, and qualify for up to a $ 500 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program ÂinsulationÂ rebate, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</foundation>
                <windows />
                <doors />
                <HotWaterSystem>

I have modeled a combination heating &amp; hot water system for this house - please see the recommendation in the Heating System section of this report.

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is heated with a combination oil boiler that has a connection (a combi loop) to also heat your domestic hot water, with a 35 gallon external storage buffer tank. To help you plan for when this system needs to be replaced, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® 95% AFUE propane combi boiler to heat all of your house and provide high efficiency on-demand domestic hot water. This system will direct vent to the outside and will no longer require the sidewall vent for the current boiler and the 5" combustion air supply pipe can also be eliminated.





Savings:

By upgrading to an ENERGY STARÂ® 95% AFUE propane combi boiler to heat all of your house and provide high efficiency on-demand domestic hot water, you could potentially save $ 882 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 21.2 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Under the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $1,600 is available for replacing an older oil or propane fired boiler with an ENERGY STAR certified propane fired boiler of the 95% efficiency that I modeled. To determine eligibility, confirm that the unit is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely with the short and dark days of winter. Although winter may provide little or no usable energy (depending on your latitude in the NWT), the long hours of sunlight in spring, summer and fall can provide significant solar heating and electricity generation opportunities for up to eight months of the year.  As Solar Photovoltaic (PV) system prices continue to drop, they become more competitive with the cost of grid-supplied and especially off-grid generation. First though - you will need to check with your Inuvik electric utility to see if they have capacity for you to connect a Solar PV system to their grid.



Savings: As a starting point for your further investigation, I modeled a 4 kW PV system, flush mounted on the front south facing roof, directly connected to the grid, without a battery-based standby power system. This is estimated to produce about 3,500 kWh annually, reducing your electricity bill by $ 1,146. This system may qualify for up to $ 20,000 from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program &amp; up to $5,000 from the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative. 



To qualify for a GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate, you must apply and receive pre-approval. The grant amount is based on the lower of:

1. 50% of the eligible cost of the system, up to a maximum of $20,000.

2. A calculation of $3.50 per watt that the system can produce.



Applicants are encouraged to contact the AEA for pre-approval prior to installation. Projects will be funded on a first come, first serve basis until the program is fully subscribed. Projects must be installed and operational to receive the rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program



Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative 



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1,300 per KW is available for installing a solar photovoltaic system technology that converts sunlight directly into electricity. If you choose to install a photovoltaic (PV) system, it is strongly recommended that a full assessment by a professional solar photovoltaic installer, including the measuring of solar irradiance (how much sunlight falls on the roof) is undertaken to provide detailed information on considerations for your home and specific installation recommendations, including the size and related energy production of the system. The rated PV panel must have peak power capacity higher than or equal to 1.0 kW. The system must be composed of photovoltaic (PV) panel and inverter certified to CSA Standards. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.



Batteries Connected to Photovoltaic Systems to Provide Standby Power for Home



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1300 is available for the installation of battery storage and an inverter to connect to a photovoltaic system to provide standby power for the home for an existing PV system or a new install. This resiliency measure must be combined with an energy efficiency retrofit from the Canada Greener Homes Grant in order to be eligible. This grant may be combined with the new PV system installation grant for a maximum grant of $5000. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.





</Renewables>
                <AdditionalComments>

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.



Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.



ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION



For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.



Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:



? In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report

? If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.

? Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit

? All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional

? It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:

     o installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*

     o an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards

     o the heat pump has been sized to your entire home

*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.

? All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.



ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES



You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.



Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources. Programs in your region that you may be eligible for include the GNWT Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) and the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, administered by the Arctic Energy Alliance. The rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

https://aea.nt.ca/program/home-improvements/

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: These programs are regularly reviewed and the rebate amounts and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights Â Non-hydro Community



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Inuvik, LED light bulb and tubes are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 400 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test = Pass



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment kitchen range hood, bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 2.2 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current gas-fired boiler is located in an exterior mechanical room and is isolated from the exhaust fans within the house ....... and therefore is not susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed and operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house: 

        - a kitchen range hood fan

        - two bathroom fans

        - a clothes dryer



You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs Â Inuvik



The following Dec. 1, 2021 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Inuvik:



Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil - $1.73 per liter 

Nat gas - $37.21 per GJ

Wood - $472.50 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $14.70 per bag, $682.50 per tonne bulk delivered

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,

Michael Goodwin, Senior Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333</AdditionalComments>
